{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/maniaclab/af-mcp-platform/pulls/158","id":4215423313,"node_id":"PR_kwDOTAYJ-c77QkFR","html_url":"https://github.com/maniaclab/af-mcp-platform/pull/158","diff_url":"https://github.com/maniaclab/af-mcp-platform/pull/158.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/maniaclab/af-mcp-platform/pull/158.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/maniaclab/af-mcp-platform/issues/158","number":158,"state":"closed","locked":false,"title":"feat(mcp): OAuth discovery + PAT bootstrap — clients authenticate without a pasted token","user":{"login":"kratsg","id":761483,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc2MTQ4Mw==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/761483?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/kratsg","html_url":"https://github.com/kratsg","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kratsg/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kratsg/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kratsg/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kratsg/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kratsg/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kratsg/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kratsg/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kratsg/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/kratsg/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Closes #140. **This removes the manual token-pasting step for interactive clients.**\n\nPoint a client at `https://mcp.af.uchicago.edu/mcp/` with no credential and it now: gets a 401 with `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer resource_metadata=\"…\"` → discovers the broker's metadata → is sent to a browser → logs in to Keycloak → and receives a PAT it uses from then on. That PAT appears in `/tokens` like any other, so it is revocable from the portal.\n\n## The architecture\n\nThe broker becomes an **OAuth-facing authorization endpoint that delegates authentication to Keycloak and issues broker-native PATs**. It is not becoming an identity provider — no passwords, MFA, account lifecycle, or login UI. OAuth does not issue the credential here; it *authorizes the broker to issue one*, which is why the PAT travels in the `access_token` field (legal — OAuth deliberately does not specify access-token format).\n\nPer #144's superseding decision, the protected-resource metadata names **the broker** as the authorization server, not the Keycloak realm. That is what makes AF Keycloak's lack of CIMD support irrelevant: clients register with the broker, which sets its own policy.\n\n## Endpoints\n- `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource` and `/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp` (RFC 9728 — clients try both forms)\n- `/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server` (RFC 8414), advertising `client_id_metadata_document_supported: true`\n- `/v1/oauth/{authorize,keycloak-login/callback,token}` — under the already-routed `/v1`, so no new ingress path needed for these\n- `/mcp`'s 401 now carries the `resource_metadata` pointer PR #145 deliberately omitted while the metadata did not yet exist\n\n## Security surface worth reviewing closely\n\n**CIMD means the broker dereferences a client-supplied URL** — a textbook SSRF vector. `cimd_client.py` requires `https`, and rejects hosts that are *or resolve to* private, loopback, link-local, multicast, reserved, or unspecified addresses, plus a self-reference check. Ported from rucio-mcp's `auth/cimd.py`, which already implements this pattern in production here.\n\nKnown residual: resolve-then-fetch is theoretically TOCTOU-vulnerable to DNS rebinding. Pinning the resolved address would close it; not done here, and worth a follow-up if the threat model warrants.\n\n**Authorization codes are single-use and TTL-bounded** (`mcp_auth_codes.py`), popped unconditionally — so a replay fails even inside the TTL window — and the PAT is minted only on redemption at `/token`, never eagerly at the Keycloak callback.\n\n**Two PKCE hops run simultaneously**: the MCP client's own challenge, carried inside the encrypted state across the Keycloak round trip, and the broker's own challenge for its login to Keycloak. `oauth_state.py` gained a sibling payload type rather than a second cipher — the existing `StatePayload` assumes a known `sub`, which a pre-authentication flow cannot supply.\n\n## The token-mint client comes back, repurposed\n\n`TOKEN_MINT_CLIENT_ID`/`SECRET` — wired in #142, then orphaned when #144 step 2a replaced RFC 8693 with PATs — now back the broker's Keycloak login client for this flow. Same env vars, same `broker.tokenMint.*` chart values, different grant type. Exactly the repurposing predicted when we decided to merge #142 rather than strip it.\n\n## Docs flipped\n`docs/connecting-a-client.md` now leads with discovery for interactive clients (just the URL, no header) and demotes manually-minted PATs to the documented fallback for CI, scripts, and headless clients.\n\n## Verification (exit codes, not greps)\n`pytest broker/ spikes/` → **662 passed** (603 baseline + 59 new), exit 0 · `lint-all` exit 0 · `check-docstrings` exit 0 · `helm lint` exit 0 across defaults, `ci/test-values.yaml`, and an override, with `helm template` confirming all three new `.well-known` paths render · docs build + linkcheck clean.\n\n## Merge note\nOpen PR #157 (capability PATs) also touches `app.py`, `config.py`, and `docs/auth.md`. Whichever merges second needs a small rebase.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n","created_at":"2026-08-05T19:53:07Z","updated_at":"2026-08-05T20:41:03Z","closed_at":"2026-08-05T20:41:01Z","merged_at":"2026-08-05T20:41:01Z","merge_commit_sha":"17df2035db4d5fdcb95ae19e9757f9d424395390","assignees":[],"requested_reviewers":[],"requested_teams":[],"labels":[],"milestone":null,"draft":false,"commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/maniaclab/af-mcp-platform/pulls/158/commits","review_comm